  "preamble": "Languages lacking /f/, or speakers who do not use it, replace it with the aspirated bilabial stop /pʰ/, which keeps voicelessness, labiality and a noisy release. Korean renders foreign /f/ as ㅍ [pʰ] (커피 [kʰʌpʰi] 'coffee'), Burmese as ဖ [pʰ] (ဖုန်း /pʰóʊɴ/ 'phone'), and rural and older Hindi speakers pronounce Perso-Arabic and English /f/ as /pʰ/ (फ़सल /fəsəl/ ~ /pʰəsəl/ 'crop'), although in Hindi the dominant modern trend runs the other way, with /f/ spreading at the expense of /pʰ/.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, Stopping, Loanword Adaptation",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Korean",
      "languageFamily": "Koreanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "English coffee /f/",
          "to": "커피 keopi [kʰʌpʰi] 'coffee'",
          "note": "Korean has no /f/; foreign /f/ is regularly adapted as the aspirated stop ㅍ."
        },
        {
          "from": "English fighting /f/",
          "to": "파이팅 paiting [pʰaitʰiŋ] 'fighting!' (cheer)",
          "note": "Pseudo-anglicism; initial /f/ adapted as [pʰ]."
        }
      ]
    },
